{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "APKGuard",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "semanticVersion": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/apkguard/apkguard",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "AG-MAN-001",
              "name": "ApplicationIsDebuggable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Application is debuggable"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "android:debuggable=\"true\" lets anyone with USB/ADB access attach a debugger, inspect memory, run code as the app (run-as) and read its private data."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "android:debuggable=\"true\" lets anyone with USB/ADB access attach a debugger, inspect memory, run code as the app (run-as) and read its private data.\n\nRemediation: Remove android:debuggable from the manifest and let the build system set it. Release builds produced by Gradle (`buildTypes.release`) are non-debuggable by default; make sure you ship a release build.",
                "markdown": "android:debuggable=\"true\" lets anyone with USB/ADB access attach a debugger, inspect memory, run code as the app (run-as) and read its private data.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove android:debuggable from the manifest and let the build system set it. Release builds produced by Gradle (`buildTypes.release`) are non-debuggable by default; make sure you ship a release build.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/application-element#debug\n- https://mas.owasp.org/MASTG/tests/android/MASVS-RESILIENCE/MASTG-TEST-0039/"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "manifest",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-489"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/application-element#debug"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-MAN-002",
              "name": "ApplicationDataBackupIsEnabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Application data backup is enabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When android:allowBackup is true (the default when the attribute is missing), the app's private files, databases and shared preferences can be extracted through cloud backup or device-to-device transfer, and on older devices via `adb backup`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When android:allowBackup is true (the default when the attribute is missing), the app's private files, databases and shared preferences can be extracted through cloud backup or device-to-device transfer, and on older devices via `adb backup`.\n\nRemediation: Set android:allowBackup=\"false\", or keep it enabled but restrict what is backed up with android:fullBackupContent (Android 11 and lower) and android:dataExtractionRules (Android 12+), excluding tokens, credentials and other sensitive files.",
                "markdown": "When android:allowBackup is true (the default when the attribute is missing), the app's private files, databases and shared preferences can be extracted through cloud backup or device-to-device transfer, and on older devices via `adb backup`.\n\n**Remediation:** Set android:allowBackup=\"false\", or keep it enabled but restrict what is backed up with android:fullBackupContent (Android 11 and lower) and android:dataExtractionRules (Android 12+), excluding tokens, credentials and other sensitive files.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup\n- https://developer.android.com/about/versions/12/backup-restore"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "manifest",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-530"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-MAN-003",
              "name": "CleartextNetworkTrafficAllowed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cleartext network traffic allowed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The app permits unencrypted HTTP traffic, either explicitly (android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\", or cleartextTrafficPermitted in the network security config) or implicitly by targeting API level 27 or lower. Traffic sent over HTTP can be read and modified by anyone on the network path."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The app permits unencrypted HTTP traffic, either explicitly (android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\", or cleartextTrafficPermitted in the network security config) or implicitly by targeting API level 27 or lower. Traffic sent over HTTP can be read and modified by anyone on the network path.\n\nRemediation: Use HTTPS for all connections. Remove android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\" and set cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\" in the network security config base-config. If a legacy host truly requires HTTP, allow it only for that domain with a <domain-config>.",
                "markdown": "The app permits unencrypted HTTP traffic, either explicitly (android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\", or cleartextTrafficPermitted in the network security config) or implicitly by targeting API level 27 or lower. Traffic sent over HTTP can be read and modified by anyone on the network path.\n\n**Remediation:** Use HTTPS for all connections. Remove android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\" and set cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\" in the network security config base-config. If a legacy host truly requires HTTP, allow it only for that domain with a <domain-config>.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-config\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/application-element#usesCleartextTraffic"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "manifest",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-319"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-config"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-MAN-004",
              "name": "ExportedComponentWithoutPermission",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exported component without permission"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An activity, service, broadcast receiver or content provider can be started or queried by any other app on the device, and no permission restricts access. Components are exported explicitly (android:exported=\"true\") or, for apps that target API level 30 or lower, implicitly by declaring an <intent-filter>. Content providers are additionally exported by default when targeting API level 16 or lower."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An activity, service, broadcast receiver or content provider can be started or queried by any other app on the device, and no permission restricts access. Components are exported explicitly (android:exported=\"true\") or, for apps that target API level 30 or lower, implicitly by declaring an <intent-filter>. Content providers are additionally exported by default when targeting API level 16 or lower.\n\nRemediation: Set android:exported=\"false\" on components that are only used inside the app. If the component must be reachable by other apps, protect it with android:permission using a signature-level custom permission, and validate all incoming intent data.",
                "markdown": "An activity, service, broadcast receiver or content provider can be started or queried by any other app on the device, and no permission restricts access. Components are exported explicitly (android:exported=\"true\") or, for apps that target API level 30 or lower, implicitly by declaring an <intent-filter>. Content providers are additionally exported by default when targeting API level 16 or lower.\n\n**Remediation:** Set android:exported=\"false\" on components that are only used inside the app. If the component must be reachable by other apps, protect it with android:permission using a signature-level custom permission, and validate all incoming intent data.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/android-exported\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/activity-element#exported"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "manifest",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-926"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/android-exported"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-MAN-005",
              "name": "OutdatedSDKVersion",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Outdated SDK version"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A low targetSdkVersion opts the app out of platform security defaults introduced in newer Android versions (e.g. cleartext blocked by default at API 28, explicit component export at API 31, safer PendingIntents and broadcast restrictions). A low minSdkVersion allows installation on devices that no longer receive security patches and lack runtime permissions (API < 23)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A low targetSdkVersion opts the app out of platform security defaults introduced in newer Android versions (e.g. cleartext blocked by default at API 28, explicit component export at API 31, safer PendingIntents and broadcast restrictions). A low minSdkVersion allows installation on devices that no longer receive security patches and lack runtime permissions (API < 23).\n\nRemediation: Raise targetSdkVersion to the level currently required by Google Play and test the behaviour changes. Consider raising minSdkVersion to at least 23 so that runtime permissions and modern TLS defaults are always available.",
                "markdown": "A low targetSdkVersion opts the app out of platform security defaults introduced in newer Android versions (e.g. cleartext blocked by default at API 28, explicit component export at API 31, safer PendingIntents and broadcast restrictions). A low minSdkVersion allows installation on devices that no longer receive security patches and lack runtime permissions (API < 23).\n\n**Remediation:** Raise targetSdkVersion to the level currently required by Google Play and test the behaviour changes. Consider raising minSdkVersion to at least 23 so that runtime permissions and modern TLS defaults are always available.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/google/play/requirements/target-sdk\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/uses-sdk-element"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "manifest",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-1104"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/google/play/requirements/target-sdk"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-PERM-001",
              "name": "DangerousOrHigh-riskPermissionsRequested",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dangerous or high-risk permissions requested"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Lists runtime (dangerous) permissions and special high-risk permissions requested by the app. Each one widens the impact of a compromise and draws user and store-review scrutiny; they should match the app's documented functionality."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Lists runtime (dangerous) permissions and special high-risk permissions requested by the app. Each one widens the impact of a compromise and draws user and store-review scrutiny; they should match the app's documented functionality.\n\nRemediation: Remove permissions that are not strictly needed (check transitive library manifests with the merged-manifest view), prefer privacy-preserving alternatives (photo picker, Storage Access Framework, coarse location) and request runtime permissions only when the feature is used.",
                "markdown": "Lists runtime (dangerous) permissions and special high-risk permissions requested by the app. Each one widens the impact of a compromise and draws user and store-review scrutiny; they should match the app's documented functionality.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove permissions that are not strictly needed (check transitive library manifests with the merged-manifest view), prefer privacy-preserving alternatives (photo picker, Storage Access Framework, coarse location) and request runtime permissions only when the feature is used.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/permissions/overview\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/minimize-permission-requests"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "permissions",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-250"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "1.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/permissions/overview"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-PERM-002",
              "name": "SuspiciousPermissionCombination",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Suspicious permission combination"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Some permissions are harmless alone but, combined, match capabilities commonly abused by spyware and banking malware (e.g. reading SMS + internet + start at boot). Legitimate apps can need them, but such combinations deserve explicit review and will be scrutinised by app-store review."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Some permissions are harmless alone but, combined, match capabilities commonly abused by spyware and banking malware (e.g. reading SMS + internet + start at boot). Legitimate apps can need them, but such combinations deserve explicit review and will be scrutinised by app-store review.\n\nRemediation: Confirm every permission in the combination is required by a user-facing feature. Remove unused ones, replace SMS access with the SMS Retriever / User Consent APIs, and document the justification for reviewers.",
                "markdown": "Some permissions are harmless alone but, combined, match capabilities commonly abused by spyware and banking malware (e.g. reading SMS + internet + start at boot). Legitimate apps can need them, but such combinations deserve explicit review and will be scrutinised by app-store review.\n\n**Remediation:** Confirm every permission in the combination is required by a user-facing feature. Remove unused ones, replace SMS access with the SMS Retriever / User Consent APIs, and document the justification for reviewers.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/permissions/default-handlers\n- https://developers.google.com/identity/sms-retriever/overview\n- https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10208820"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "permissions",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-250"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/permissions/default-handlers"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-001",
              "name": "DynamicCodeLoading",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dynamic code loading"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The app loads Dalvik bytecode at runtime (DexClassLoader, InMemoryDexClassLoader, DexFile, PathClassLoader). Code that is not part of the signed APK escapes store review and static analysis; if it is downloaded or stored in a writable location, an attacker who can replace the file gains code execution in the app."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The app loads Dalvik bytecode at runtime (DexClassLoader, InMemoryDexClassLoader, DexFile, PathClassLoader). Code that is not part of the signed APK escapes store review and static analysis; if it is downloaded or stored in a writable location, an attacker who can replace the file gains code execution in the app.\n\nRemediation: Avoid loading code at runtime; use Play Feature Delivery for modular features. If unavoidable, load only from the app's private, read-only storage, verify a cryptographic signature of the file before loading, and never load from external storage or over plain HTTP.",
                "markdown": "The app loads Dalvik bytecode at runtime (DexClassLoader, InMemoryDexClassLoader, DexFile, PathClassLoader). Code that is not part of the signed APK escapes store review and static analysis; if it is downloaded or stored in a writable location, an attacker who can replace the file gains code execution in the app.\n\n**Remediation:** Avoid loading code at runtime; use Play Feature Delivery for modular features. If unavoidable, load only from the app's private, read-only storage, verify a cryptographic signature of the file before loading, and never load from external storage or over plain HTTP.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/dynamic-code-loading\n- https://mas.owasp.org/MASTG/knowledge/android/MASVS-CODE/MASTG-KNOW-0026/"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-829"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/dynamic-code-loading"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-002",
              "name": "Reflection-heavyCode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflection-heavy code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Classes that make many reflective calls (Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod, Method.invoke, setAccessible), especially with names that are not compile-time constants, hide their behaviour from static analysis and are a common obfuscation technique. Reflection is also used to reach hidden platform APIs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Classes that make many reflective calls (Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod, Method.invoke, setAccessible), especially with names that are not compile-time constants, hide their behaviour from static analysis and are a common obfuscation technique. Reflection is also used to reach hidden platform APIs.\n\nRemediation: Prefer direct, type-safe calls. Where reflection is needed (e.g. optional dependencies), use constant class/member names, never build them from external input, and avoid accessing non-SDK (hidden) interfaces.",
                "markdown": "Classes that make many reflective calls (Class.forName, getDeclaredMethod, Method.invoke, setAccessible), especially with names that are not compile-time constants, hide their behaviour from static analysis and are a common obfuscation technique. Reflection is also used to reach hidden platform APIs.\n\n**Remediation:** Prefer direct, type-safe calls. Where reflection is needed (e.g. optional dependencies), use constant class/member names, never build them from external input, and avoid accessing non-SDK (hidden) interfaces.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/guide/app-compatibility/restrictions-non-sdk-interfaces\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-470"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "3.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/guide/app-compatibility/restrictions-non-sdk-interfaces"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-003",
              "name": "WebViewJavaScriptInterfaceExposed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "WebView JavaScript interface exposed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "WebView.addJavascriptInterface exposes Java objects to JavaScript running in the page. Any script that runs in that WebView - including injected or third-party content, or content loaded over HTTP - can call the exposed methods. On API level < 17 every public method (including getClass()) is reachable, which leads to remote code execution."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "WebView.addJavascriptInterface exposes Java objects to JavaScript running in the page. Any script that runs in that WebView - including injected or third-party content, or content loaded over HTTP - can call the exposed methods. On API level < 17 every public method (including getClass()) is reachable, which leads to remote code execution.\n\nRemediation: Load only trusted, HTTPS content in WebViews that have a JavaScript interface. Expose a minimal object whose methods are annotated with @JavascriptInterface, validate all arguments, and consider WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener with an origin allow-list instead. Set minSdkVersion >= 17.",
                "markdown": "WebView.addJavascriptInterface exposes Java objects to JavaScript running in the page. Any script that runs in that WebView - including injected or third-party content, or content loaded over HTTP - can call the exposed methods. On API level < 17 every public method (including getClass()) is reachable, which leads to remote code execution.\n\n**Remediation:** Load only trusted, HTTPS content in WebViews that have a JavaScript interface. Expose a minimal object whose methods are annotated with @JavascriptInterface, validate all arguments, and consider WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener with an origin allow-list instead. Set minSdkVersion >= 17.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/insecure-webview-native-bridges\n- https://developer.android.com/reference/android/webkit/WebView#addJavascriptInterface(java.lang.Object,%20java.lang.String)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-749"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/insecure-webview-native-bridges"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-004",
              "name": "InsecureWebViewConfiguration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure WebView configuration"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Detects WebView settings that weaken the same-origin policy for file:// URLs or allow mixed content, enabled JavaScript (informational), and WebViewClient.onReceivedSslError handlers that call SslErrorHandler.proceed(), which silently accepts invalid TLS certificates and enables man-in-the-middle attacks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Detects WebView settings that weaken the same-origin policy for file:// URLs or allow mixed content, enabled JavaScript (informational), and WebViewClient.onReceivedSslError handlers that call SslErrorHandler.proceed(), which silently accepts invalid TLS certificates and enables man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\nRemediation: Keep setAllowFileAccessFromFileURLs and setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs false (the default since API 16), use MIXED_CONTENT_NEVER_ALLOW, load local content via WebViewAssetLoader, and never call handler.proceed() in onReceivedSslError - call handler.cancel() instead.",
                "markdown": "Detects WebView settings that weaken the same-origin policy for file:// URLs or allow mixed content, enabled JavaScript (informational), and WebViewClient.onReceivedSslError handlers that call SslErrorHandler.proceed(), which silently accepts invalid TLS certificates and enables man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\n**Remediation:** Keep setAllowFileAccessFromFileURLs and setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs false (the default since API 16), use MIXED_CONTENT_NEVER_ALLOW, load local content via WebViewAssetLoader, and never call handler.proceed() in onReceivedSslError - call handler.cancel() instead.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/webview-unsafe-file-inclusion\n- https://developer.android.com/reference/android/webkit/WebViewClient#onReceivedSslError(android.webkit.WebView,%20android.webkit.SslErrorHandler,%20android.net.http.SslError)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-295"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/webview-unsafe-file-inclusion"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-005",
              "name": "WeakCipherOrCipherMode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Weak cipher or cipher mode"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Cipher.getInstance is called with a broken algorithm (DES, 3DES, RC4, Blowfish, RC2), with ECB mode, with a bare block-cipher name such as \"AES\" (which defaults to ECB), or with unpadded RSA. These do not provide confidentiality against a capable attacker."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Cipher.getInstance is called with a broken algorithm (DES, 3DES, RC4, Blowfish, RC2), with ECB mode, with a bare block-cipher name such as \"AES\" (which defaults to ECB), or with unpadded RSA. These do not provide confidentiality against a capable attacker.\n\nRemediation: Use authenticated encryption: \"AES/GCM/NoPadding\" with a random 12-byte IV per message (or ChaCha20-Poly1305), and RSA only with OAEP padding. Consider Google Tink or Jetpack Security, which choose safe parameters for you.",
                "markdown": "Cipher.getInstance is called with a broken algorithm (DES, 3DES, RC4, Blowfish, RC2), with ECB mode, with a bare block-cipher name such as \"AES\" (which defaults to ECB), or with unpadded RSA. These do not provide confidentiality against a capable attacker.\n\n**Remediation:** Use authenticated encryption: \"AES/GCM/NoPadding\" with a random 12-byte IV per message (or ChaCha20-Poly1305), and RSA only with OAEP padding. Consider Google Tink or Jetpack Security, which choose safe parameters for you.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/cryptography\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/weak-prng\n- https://mas.owasp.org/MASTG/tests/android/MASVS-CRYPTO/MASTG-TEST-0013/"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-327"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/cryptography"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-006",
              "name": "WeakHashAlgorithm",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Weak hash algorithm"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "MD5, MD4, MD2 and SHA-1 are vulnerable to collision attacks and must not be used for signatures, integrity protection of untrusted data, or password hashing. Usage for non-security purposes (cache keys, checksums) is acceptable but should be confirmed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "MD5, MD4, MD2 and SHA-1 are vulnerable to collision attacks and must not be used for signatures, integrity protection of untrusted data, or password hashing. Usage for non-security purposes (cache keys, checksums) is acceptable but should be confirmed.\n\nRemediation: Use SHA-256 or stronger for integrity, HMAC-SHA256 for message authentication, and a dedicated password hashing function (Argon2, scrypt, PBKDF2 with a high iteration count) for passwords. Suppress the finding if the usage is not security relevant.",
                "markdown": "MD5, MD4, MD2 and SHA-1 are vulnerable to collision attacks and must not be used for signatures, integrity protection of untrusted data, or password hashing. Usage for non-security purposes (cache keys, checksums) is acceptable but should be confirmed.\n\n**Remediation:** Use SHA-256 or stronger for integrity, HMAC-SHA256 for message authentication, and a dedicated password hashing function (Argon2, scrypt, PBKDF2 with a high iteration count) for passwords. Suppress the finding if the usage is not security relevant.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/cryptography#deprecated-functionality\n- https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/328.html"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-328"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/cryptography#deprecated-functionality"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-007",
              "name": "HardcodedCryptographicKeyOrIV",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded cryptographic key or IV"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Key material is built from a constant embedded in the app (a string literal converted with getBytes()/toCharArray()/Base64.decode(), or an array literal). Anyone can extract it from the APK and decrypt or forge data protected by it. A constant IV or nonce breaks the security of CBC/GCM encryption."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Key material is built from a constant embedded in the app (a string literal converted with getBytes()/toCharArray()/Base64.decode(), or an array literal). Anyone can extract it from the APK and decrypt or forge data protected by it. A constant IV or nonce breaks the security of CBC/GCM encryption.\n\nRemediation: Generate keys at runtime and store them in the Android Keystore (KeyGenParameterSpec), or derive them from user input with a salted KDF. Generate a fresh random IV/nonce for every encryption with SecureRandom and store it alongside the ciphertext.",
                "markdown": "Key material is built from a constant embedded in the app (a string literal converted with getBytes()/toCharArray()/Base64.decode(), or an array literal). Anyone can extract it from the APK and decrypt or forge data protected by it. A constant IV or nonce breaks the security of CBC/GCM encryption.\n\n**Remediation:** Generate keys at runtime and store them in the Android Keystore (KeyGenParameterSpec), or derive them from user input with a salted KDF. Generate a fresh random IV/nonce for every encryption with SecureRandom and store it alongside the ciphertext.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/keystore\n- https://mas.owasp.org/MASTG/tests/android/MASVS-CRYPTO/MASTG-TEST-0013/"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-321"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/keystore"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-008",
              "name": "InsecureTrustManager",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure TrustManager"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A custom X509TrustManager implements checkServerTrusted with an empty body, so every server certificate - including self-signed or attacker-issued ones - is accepted. Any network attacker can intercept and modify TLS traffic."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A custom X509TrustManager implements checkServerTrusted with an empty body, so every server certificate - including self-signed or attacker-issued ones - is accepted. Any network attacker can intercept and modify TLS traffic.\n\nRemediation: Remove the custom TrustManager and rely on the platform default. To trust a private CA or pin certificates, declare it in the network security config (<trust-anchors>, <pin-set>) instead of writing TLS validation code.",
                "markdown": "A custom X509TrustManager implements checkServerTrusted with an empty body, so every server certificate - including self-signed or attacker-issued ones - is accepted. Any network attacker can intercept and modify TLS traffic.\n\n**Remediation:** Remove the custom TrustManager and rely on the platform default. To trust a private CA or pin certificates, declare it in the network security config (<trust-anchors>, <pin-set>) instead of writing TLS validation code.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/unsafe-trustmanager\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-ssl"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-295"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/unsafe-trustmanager"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-CODE-009",
              "name": "InsecureHostnameVerifier",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure HostnameVerifier"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A HostnameVerifier whose verify() always returns true, or the deprecated ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER / AllowAllHostnameVerifier, disables hostname checks: a valid certificate for *any* domain is accepted, so TLS connections can be intercepted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A HostnameVerifier whose verify() always returns true, or the deprecated ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER / AllowAllHostnameVerifier, disables hostname checks: a valid certificate for *any* domain is accepted, so TLS connections can be intercepted.\n\nRemediation: Do not override hostname verification. Remove the custom HostnameVerifier and use the default (HttpsURLConnection.getDefaultHostnameVerifier() / OkHttp's OkHostnameVerifier).",
                "markdown": "A HostnameVerifier whose verify() always returns true, or the deprecated ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER / AllowAllHostnameVerifier, disables hostname checks: a valid certificate for *any* domain is accepted, so TLS connections can be intercepted.\n\n**Remediation:** Do not override hostname verification. Remove the custom HostnameVerifier and use the default (HttpsURLConnection.getDefaultHostnameVerifier() / OkHttp's OkHostnameVerifier).\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/unsafe-hostname\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-ssl#CommonHostnameProbs"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-297"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/unsafe-hostname"
            },
            {
              "id": "AG-SEC-001",
              "name": "HardcodedSecret",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded secret"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An API key, access token, private key or credential is embedded in the app's code, resources or assets. APKs are trivially unpacked, so anything shipped inside them must be considered public."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An API key, access token, private key or credential is embedded in the app's code, resources or assets. APKs are trivially unpacked, so anything shipped inside them must be considered public.\n\nRemediation: Revoke and rotate the exposed credential. Keep secrets on your backend and have the app obtain short-lived, user-scoped tokens at runtime. For keys that must ship in the client (e.g. Google Maps), restrict them by package name and signing certificate in the provider console. Use a secrets plugin to keep keys out of source control.",
                "markdown": "An API key, access token, private key or credential is embedded in the app's code, resources or assets. APKs are trivially unpacked, so anything shipped inside them must be considered public.\n\n**Remediation:** Revoke and rotate the exposed credential. Keep secrets on your backend and have the app obtain short-lived, user-scoped tokens at runtime. For keys that must ship in the client (e.g. Google Maps), restrict them by package name and signing certificate in the provider console. Use a secrets plugin to keep keys out of source control.\n\n**References:**\n- https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/hardcoded-cryptographic-secrets\n- https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/api-keys#securing\n- https://mas.owasp.org/MASTG/tests/android/MASVS-STORAGE/MASTG-TEST-0001/"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "secrets",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-798"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/risks/hardcoded-cryptographic-secrets"
            }
          ]
        }
